HOW TO DESIGN:
1. Decide what arrangement serves the request and purpose (e.g. workstations with chairs at desks, storage rows with aisles, a staggered obstacle course a robot must weave through, clear floor for testing). Use the room the way the request implies: storage spreads over the floor in rows with aisles, clutter is scattered over the area, a course uses the hall's length; do not pack everything into one corner. Think like the person who asked.
2. Choose the REQUIREMENTS that capture what the user asked for, so the checker can verify it. Always checked anyway: every id placed, inside the room, no overlaps, doorways clear. Add a check for every functional relationship: a chair belongs at a desk or table -> in_front_of (chair, desk), which also requires the chair to face it; a screen on a desk -> set its "on" to that desk and add faces (screen, the chair at that desk); robots must get through -> path_between_doors. Do not tie together objects that have no functional relationship. Optional checks:
{% for name, spec in catalogue.items() %}
   - {"check": "{{ name }}", {{ spec[0] }}}: {{ spec[1] }}
{% endfor %}
   Robots need about 0.8 m wide passages; people about 0.9 m.
3. Give every object x, y, yaw. Only small items resting on top of furniture (a monitor or lamp on a desk) set "on" to the supporting id and sit inside its footprint; floor furniture such as chairs never uses "on". For an irregular arrangement you cannot lay out by hand (clutter, debris, randomly strewn obstacles), list those ids in "scatter" with the area to cover: the code puts them at random free spots there, randomly turned, at least "gap" m apart (their x/y in "objects" are then ignored). Only for arrangements with no order: anything with a pattern (a slalom, rows, a line, a ring) you lay out yourself.
4. State each model's front (the side a person uses or faces from: a chair's seat side, opposite its backrest; a desk's or table's user side, which is one of its LONG sides; a shelf's open side) in "fronts" as one of "+x", "-x", "+y", "-y" in the model's own axes, e.g. {"chair": "-y"}. Sizes above are width (local x) x length (local y), so the long sides of a 0.5 x 0.84 m desk are +x/-x. The checker uses fronts for "faces" and "in_front_of"; the default is "+x".{% if known_fronts %} Fronts measured from the models' geometry: {% for type, front in known_fronts.items() %}{{ type }} {{ front }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %} (use these; do not redeclare them).{% endif %}{% if no_front %} These models have no front (symmetric or usable from any side): {{ no_front | join(", ") }}; never use them as "a" in faces; as "b" in in_front_of any of their sides counts (chairs around a table).{% endif %}{% if has_views %} Work it out from the MODEL VIEWS panel: each model is drawn alone at yaw 0 from above with its +x (red) and +y (green) axes; darker dots are taller parts, so a chair's backrest (dark band) marks its back and its front is the opposite side.{% endif %}

